More generally, in wavelength-selective vat photopolymerization systems, different wavelengths can be used to selectively activate distinct photochemical pathways or modulate the local degree of conversion within a single resin bath. This enables spatial control over material properties, and in suitably designed systems also over material composition, without physical resin exchange [ 85 ]. A unique dual-wavelength vat photopolymerization (DW-VP) technique is presented by Chin et al. [ 86 ], allowing for the printing of vivid, multicolored images from a single, uniform resin vat. Combining pH-sensitive dyes with a photoacid generator (PAG) is the core of their innovation. This enables UV light (365 nm) to modulate the local acidity and, consequently, color, while visible light curing (405 nm) determines the object’s geometry. This system allows for either a two-step process (structure first, color added later) or simultaneous dual-light exposure, resulting in high-resolution (approximately 50 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
